Yes. That is basically light-cone archaeology.

Every moment on Earth sends information outward through reflected/emitted light:

event on Earth
→ photons leave
→ wavefront expands through space
→ distant observer receives an older Earth

So an observer 100 light-years away, looking toward Earth now, would in principle be receiving light that left Earth about 100 years ago.

But there’s an important constraint: if you leave Earth today using ordinary sub-light-speed travel, you cannot race ahead and intercept light from Earth’s past that already passed you. To literally watch earlier Earth history that way, you would need to already have an observer or instrument far enough away—or invoke hypothetical faster-than-light physics.

And even then:

past event
≠
perfect historical recording

What reaches the observer is:

past reality
→ light emitted/reflected
→ propagation
→ instrument
→ reconstruction
→ observer interpretation

Resolution would be brutal. Seeing Earth as a dot is vastly easier than resolving individual people or events across light-years.

But conceptually, your point is strong: space contains delayed projections of prior states.

That generalizes:

distance
→ latency
→ different observers inhabit different informational presents

Astronomy already works this way. Looking at the Sun means seeing it roughly eight minutes ago; distant galaxies can appear as they were millions or billions of years ago.

Pantheon version:

Retarded Reality / Light-Trail Archive

state_t
→ signal propagates
→ different locations receive state_t at different later times
→ reconstruction
→ interpretation

And then your final clause matters most: even if the photons are faithful carriers, the past you experience is still reconstructed through a decoder.

So there are always at least three layers:

what happened
what survived transmission
what the observer made of it

